WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:05.480
Today, we are welcoming back to the program
Congressman Rokaya representing California's

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:09.690
17th district, located in the heart of Silicon
Valley.

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:13.690
Congressman, always great to have you on.
Great to be on.

00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:19.900
So let's just start with the covid relief
bill, popular with 76 percent of Americans,

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:26.289
even 60 percent of Republicans.
Rarely do you see such agreement among three

00:00:26.289 --> 00:00:31.310
hundred and thirty million Americans on anything,
one point nine dollars trillion dollars. Many

00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:35.070
people wondering, what what are we waiting
on right now exactly?

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:39.870
Well, we're going to pass it this week in
the House and then it goes to the Senate,

00:00:39.870 --> 00:00:45.199
so we're waiting as often on the Senate and
most of the bill is going to go through. The

00:00:45.199 --> 00:00:50.379
big debate, as you know, is whether the minimum
wage will be part of the increase to fifteen

00:00:50.379 --> 00:00:55.820
dollars.
Is at this point, do you think that understanding

00:00:55.820 --> 00:01:02.630
how popular the bill is nationally, that Republicans
in the Senate are really going to stand in

00:01:02.630 --> 00:01:05.470
the way?
Or will some of them take the more pragmatic

00:01:05.470 --> 00:01:09.940
approach of realizing that this is popular
even with many of their constituents, and

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:13.940
it makes sense to to be on the right side,
so to speak?

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:17.690
You would think that, but that's not the indication,
I mean, when you have Mitt Romney coming out

00:01:17.690 --> 00:01:22.770
saying that this is too big of bill and too
much spending, I mean, if he's not going to

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:27.190
be for it doesn't give me much hope that you're
going to get many Republicans for it. So I

00:01:27.190 --> 00:01:30.510
expect, unfortunately, that this will be a
straight party line vote.

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:37.990
How soon would the stimulus deposits and or
checks start getting to people if it was passed

00:01:37.990 --> 00:01:41.970
sometime next week?
It would be a few weeks typically, I mean,

00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:47.240
I know in the previous administration, it
dragged on for months and we still have some

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:52.650
people who are asking about the six hundred
dollar check from last time. So I hope we've

00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:56.450
learned from that. Typically, it takes about
two to three weeks for people to see the check

00:01:56.450 --> 00:02:01.280
after the president signs it.
In your mind, would this be the last covid

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:05.420
stimulus?
I hope not. I have called Tim Ryan since the

00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:10.209
economic crisis began four monthly checks
because people's rent comes due every month

00:02:10.209 --> 00:02:15.260
or a lot of folks who aren't getting unemployment
benefits. I think we have to continue until

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:21.730
the crisis is over, until our economy is fully
open again. And I'm going to continue to push

00:02:21.730 --> 00:02:25.230
for that.
What metrics would you look at to determine

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:29.099
when that is?
I guess I would look at the unemployment rate,

00:02:29.099 --> 00:02:34.250
but not just the unemployment rate for people
who have opted out of the workforce. I would

00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:40.439
look at the infection rate and the prevalence
and seeing how much we've been able to open

00:02:40.439 --> 00:02:45.290
up our economy.
Again, the 15 dollar minimum wage, which is

00:02:45.290 --> 00:02:51.269
part of the version that is currently in your
chamber in the House of Representatives, if

00:02:51.269 --> 00:02:56.060
that doesn't ultimately get included in the
bill, is that the next thing you'd like to

00:02:56.060 --> 00:02:58.909
see Congress take up or is there something
else?

00:02:58.909 --> 00:03:03.389
I would, because it's the one thing that represents
structural change. So the bill is very important.

00:03:03.389 --> 00:03:07.799
It gets money to state and local governments.
It gives money for vaccines. It gets money

00:03:07.799 --> 00:03:12.870
of cash for every individual. But it's not
structurally changing the economy. The minimum

00:03:12.870 --> 00:03:17.930
wage does not fundamentally increase wages
for people. The challenges and Bernie Sanders

00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:22.760
made this point yesterday that you can't do
the minimum wage without reconciliation. They're

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:26.799
just not 60 votes. So, yeah, you either have
to get rid of the filibuster or you have to

00:03:26.799 --> 00:03:32.329
do reconciliation, which is why it's so important
to get this in the current bill.

00:03:32.329 --> 00:03:38.010
When we talk about the immigration situation
we covered on the program earlier this week

00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:47.260
how Joe Biden did very quickly do this executive
order to stop the removal of undocumented

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:54.359
immigrants back to a number of different countries.
But a judge very quickly kiboshed that. And

00:03:54.359 --> 00:04:00.030
since correct me if I'm wrong, Joe Biden has
not attempted with a different legal justification

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:03.779
to do another executive order. And I've read
a number of interesting legal assessments

00:04:03.779 --> 00:04:08.209
that say if Joe Biden was really determined
to do this, there are a number of other ways

00:04:08.209 --> 00:04:15.930
that he could word and structure executive
orders. The it was it was very narrowly stopped

00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:21.470
by this Texas judge. And Joe Biden hasn't
done that. I've said that. I think that if

00:04:21.470 --> 00:04:25.410
indeed this is the priority, Joe Biden said
it would be in that I believe it should be

00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:29.731
he should be going right back at this with
another executive order and another. What

00:04:29.731 --> 00:04:34.390
do you think?
I agree with you. I mean, I think that we

00:04:34.390 --> 00:04:39.810
shouldn't let one judge stop the process of
having a more humane immigration policy. And

00:04:39.810 --> 00:04:45.010
the biggest concern to me are the kids. And
I'm sure you read also on the other detention

00:04:45.010 --> 00:04:51.690
and sending them there. Now, there are alternatives.
You can have community detention, community

00:04:51.690 --> 00:04:57.200
placement, and it's a question of having the
resources in a plant. So I think that this

00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:02.280
has to be looked at. I've looked I'm on Pramila
Jampolis road map for immigration. She's going

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:08.600
to be putting out a plan of how we keep our
borders safe, but are not treating most of

00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:11.630
the migrants coming over, particularly kids,
as criminals.

00:05:11.630 --> 00:05:15.700
So if Joe if Joe Biden doesn't do it, if he
just leaves it, as I did an executive order

00:05:15.700 --> 00:05:21.340
and a judge stopped it and I guess now we
have to wait, would you consider that a failure?

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:26.370
I don't think that's true to what he ran on.
I mean, we every Democratic candidate running

00:05:26.370 --> 00:05:33.440
said that we were going to look at the inhumane
Trump policy and return to a policy of not

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:39.150
criminalizing most people crossing the border,
that it is a civil infraction and you can

00:05:39.150 --> 00:05:47.160
be sent back. But there has to be a move that
we are treating mothers and kids as criminals

00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:53.460
and that we're placing them in community placements
instead of these detention centers.

00:05:53.460 --> 00:06:00.020
In terms of the makeup of the House of Representatives
right now, when we look at national polling

00:06:00.020 --> 00:06:05.250
among Republican voters, for the most part,
the support of the former President Trump

00:06:05.250 --> 00:06:10.120
is is still significant. Something like 80
percent of Republicans want him to remain

00:06:10.120 --> 00:06:16.030
involved, 64 percent said if he split off
and started his own party that they would

00:06:16.030 --> 00:06:20.370
follow him. Now, I expect that in practice
those numbers would be lower. I think the

00:06:20.370 --> 00:06:25.521
idea of splitting off is more appealing than
actually doing it. But needless to say, for

00:06:25.521 --> 00:06:29.250
the time being, there's continued support
among the Republican Party for the former

00:06:29.250 --> 00:06:35.250
president. What is your assessment right now
of the impact within your legislative body,

00:06:35.250 --> 00:06:40.090
of the presence of folks like Marjorie Taylor
Green and Lauren Bobert and Madison Cawthorn,

00:06:40.090 --> 00:06:44.950
who arguably are only even there by riding
on the coattails of Trump ism in the last

00:06:44.950 --> 00:06:49.750
six months of 2020?
Well, they've been marginalized from the body,

00:06:49.750 --> 00:06:53.940
but the challenge is that there were almost
one hundred and forty Republicans who voted

00:06:53.940 --> 00:07:00.300
for the the opposing the certification of
the election. And that's a lot of that's not

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:04.930
just some of these extreme cases. And so the
decision by the Republican Party has to make

00:07:04.930 --> 00:07:10.320
and it's a very difficult decision, is how
do they move beyond Trump without abdicating

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:15.181
their base? And I don't think any Republican
actually has figured that out. I mean, you

00:07:15.181 --> 00:07:19.790
have Nikki Haley taking a different side of
the equation every day because they're trying

00:07:19.790 --> 00:07:25.530
that balancing act. But and they've seen the
consequences of a Liz Cheney or someone who

00:07:25.530 --> 00:07:33.210
tries a clean break. And until they are able
to get a coherent vision of a post Trump future,

00:07:33.210 --> 00:07:38.010
they're going to struggle.
Is it naive to think that this fracturing,

00:07:38.010 --> 00:07:43.180
particularly if this divide becomes more noticeable,
if Donald Trump does start his own party or

00:07:43.180 --> 00:07:48.010
if he announces at CPAC on Sunday that he's
running as a Republican in 2020 for any of

00:07:48.010 --> 00:07:52.000
these things that might fracture these two
sides, is it naive for me to think that it's

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:57.110
an opportunity for the left to get things
done while they are fighting among themselves?

00:07:57.110 --> 00:08:03.180
Well, I I think for the sake of the country,
it would be better to have a coherent party

00:08:03.180 --> 00:08:09.360
that rejects racism and that moves beyond
Trump. But what I would say is we have all

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:13.710
the tools we need, but we don't even have
the focus right now on the Republicans. When

00:08:13.710 --> 00:08:18.140
we have to do is focus on how controlling
the House, controlling the Senate or the presidency.

00:08:18.140 --> 00:08:23.900
We get our things through and we ought to
be doing aggressively through reconciliation,

00:08:23.900 --> 00:08:30.240
the fifty dollar wage that covid relief infrastructure
and expanding health care. So I think we have

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:34.930
an opportunity to deliver on our agenda on
health care.

00:08:34.930 --> 00:08:41.539
What do you see as as the next step? Not in
a in a perfect world where we might be talking

00:08:41.539 --> 00:08:46.740
about Medicare for all or something else,
but based on what Joe Biden ran on, is it

00:08:46.740 --> 00:08:52.200
is it a simple let's establish a public option?
What is it that you see as the immediate next

00:08:52.200 --> 00:08:55.270
step on health care?
Well, we should expand Medicare, and he ran

00:08:55.270 --> 00:09:01.779
on expanding it to 60. That seems to me an
easy left. We should look at how we at least

00:09:01.779 --> 00:09:11.000
get a public option that is affordable as
a step towards a single payer. I mean, I believe

00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:16.180
fundamentally Medicare for all is the right
solution. But what I would say is, let's look

00:09:16.180 --> 00:09:22.470
at any step in the lens of getting us closer
to that ultimate goal and we should support

00:09:22.470 --> 00:09:26.209
those parts that are helping us move in that
direction.

00:09:26.209 --> 00:09:30.490
In your mind, does this have to be done before
twenty, twenty two because we simply don't

00:09:30.490 --> 00:09:36.709
know who will control what after that point?
I do. I mean, I think it has to be done not

00:09:36.709 --> 00:09:40.149
for the sake of politics or the bill itself.
You don't do this, you'll lose the House.

00:09:40.149 --> 00:09:44.129
I just think it's so rare in American politics
that you have the presidency, the Senate and

00:09:44.129 --> 00:09:49.970
the House. And if we don't do this, who knows
when all those stars align? Again, we're working

00:09:49.970 --> 00:09:53.450
very, very hard to make sure we keep the House
and Senate chambers working hard to keep the

00:09:53.450 --> 00:09:57.569
Senate. And I think we have a very good chance.
But politics is unpredictable.

00:09:57.569 --> 00:10:02.339
And while we have this opportunity, let's
deliver on some structural change to improve

00:10:02.339 --> 00:10:07.779
people's lives in terms of the day to day
in the House, you know, we've had these stories

00:10:07.779 --> 00:10:14.529
about metal detectors and we've learned about
Congressman Cawthorn saying that with his

00:10:14.529 --> 00:10:19.360
wheelchair, he's able to actually have multiple
firearms with him. And it's not obvious to

00:10:19.360 --> 00:10:26.149
others that that that's what's going on. Are
you feeling worried day to day about firearms

00:10:26.149 --> 00:10:30.110
at the Capitol?
I personally am not. But I feel I worry for

00:10:30.110 --> 00:10:34.220
our colleagues. I worry for some of the women
of color who have been subject to many death

00:10:34.220 --> 00:10:42.959
threats. I worry for people who have been
outspoken and targets on on right wing media.

00:10:42.959 --> 00:10:46.990
And then I'm sad for our country that every
time I go to the Capitol, it's basically a

00:10:46.990 --> 00:10:52.490
fortress. Barbed wire. You have the National
Guard. I mean, it's a sad thing that we've

00:10:52.490 --> 00:10:58.399
created that distance between government and
people because of the threats that are there

00:10:58.399 --> 00:11:03.350
on the building.
So you're not necessarily changing your protocol,

00:11:03.350 --> 00:11:08.491
but much has changed at the Capitol.
Yeah, I mean, everyone is more vigilant. That's

00:11:08.491 --> 00:11:14.259
just common sense, and me and our team are
more vigilant. We probably lock the doors

00:11:14.259 --> 00:11:19.301
more. We're more careful. We're making sure
that we're not taking unnecessary risks. But

00:11:19.301 --> 00:11:24.779
personally, I don't feel threatened in the
way that some of my colleagues, unfortunately,

00:11:24.779 --> 00:11:29.279
have been threatened.
Yeah, you mentioned infrastructure. And this

00:11:29.279 --> 00:11:34.339
is such a big topic now, particularly in light
of what happened in Texas last week. And,

00:11:34.339 --> 00:11:40.370
of course, we've gone into detail about the
way the Texas grid works. The reality of it's

00:11:40.370 --> 00:11:46.470
very plausible to Winterreise wind turbines,
wind turbines are not the cause of this issue.

00:11:46.470 --> 00:11:52.110
But can you talk a little bit about what would
a green infrastructure deal look like? What

00:11:52.110 --> 00:11:57.019
would be the most important things to include
in terms of the types of subsidies maybe that

00:11:57.019 --> 00:12:00.820
would be put in place, the types of jobs that
we should be looking to create, how we should

00:12:00.820 --> 00:12:04.959
be managing the reality that for the most
part, we still do get most of our energy from

00:12:04.959 --> 00:12:09.689
fossil fuels at this point in time.
Well, David, I chair the subcommittee on the

00:12:09.689 --> 00:12:13.630
Environment, and we're going to be holding
a hearing on what happened in Texas and you're

00:12:13.630 --> 00:12:16.569
absolutely right with one of the questions
we're going to ask, whether this is actually

00:12:16.569 --> 00:12:23.439
cheaper to weatherize wind turbines than it
is to weatherize natural gas pipeline. So

00:12:23.439 --> 00:12:29.560
shouldn't this incident have us move towards
renewable energy? That's either easier to

00:12:29.560 --> 00:12:33.790
weatherize, that's easier to build infrastructure.
And we need to be making that kind of investment

00:12:33.790 --> 00:12:39.290
in renewable energy generation, in battery
storage and electric vehicle. And all of these

00:12:39.290 --> 00:12:42.779
things are going to create jobs. And we ought
to have the federal government help finance

00:12:42.779 --> 00:12:48.449
it or take equity in it, because these are
large capital projects in building the modern

00:12:48.449 --> 00:12:55.220
infrastructure for the new energy economy.
That is something that's jobs, good jobs,

00:12:55.220 --> 00:12:58.490
and it's also something that's going to help
us tackle climate change.

00:12:58.490 --> 00:13:03.511
What will it ultimately take, though, to really
signal a sea change in terms of I mean, your

00:13:03.511 --> 00:13:09.610
state of California has taken a pretty definitive
position on a new new vehicle. Sales will

00:13:09.610 --> 00:13:16.279
have to be electric after. Correct me if I'm
wrong, is it 20, 35? Yes. Some European countries,

00:13:16.279 --> 00:13:20.860
Germany, for example, has taken a similar
approach, but at the national level rather

00:13:20.860 --> 00:13:25.249
than at the state level, do you think ultimately
the federal government is going to have to

00:13:25.249 --> 00:13:28.200
make some of those decisions in order to really
move forward?

00:13:28.200 --> 00:13:32.770
I do. I think we ought to set ourselves. I
think those standards will be good for American

00:13:32.770 --> 00:13:37.440
business. It's good for us to lead in electric
vehicles rather than allowing China to lead.

00:13:37.440 --> 00:13:42.769
And we could create the incentives to build
those plants in places that have lost manufacturing.

00:13:42.769 --> 00:13:48.369
For example, Lordstown, Lordstown, Ohio, lost
an auto plant because we have a demand for

00:13:48.369 --> 00:13:54.879
SUVs over over sedans. Well, why don't we
incentivize that? Lordstown Lordstown, Ohio,

00:13:54.879 --> 00:13:59.910
could become an electric vehicle plant manufacturer
or a battery manufacturer. One of the things

00:13:59.910 --> 00:14:04.929
that's interesting is a lot of the component
parts of electric vehicles could be made in

00:14:04.929 --> 00:14:11.499
the United States. So we have to have a real
view of how we reindustrialize America. And

00:14:11.499 --> 00:14:17.139
if we do that, it can be towards climate change,
tackling it, but also towards job creation.

00:14:17.139 --> 00:14:23.060
Yeah, there's a bit of a myopic view on electric
cars. I mean, certainly right now Tesla is

00:14:23.060 --> 00:14:27.809
the lion's share of the market and those are
American vehicles. It's unbelievable what's

00:14:27.809 --> 00:14:32.829
going on. But when you look at Revy and you
look at Polestar, you look at what clearly

00:14:32.829 --> 00:14:39.100
is a growing presence of Chinese electric
vehicles. There's a particular window of opportunity

00:14:39.100 --> 00:14:43.889
right now that it sounds like you're saying
should should be seized before this goes elsewhere.

00:14:43.889 --> 00:14:48.369
Exactly, and China currently leads and the
electric vehicle market, I mean, they don't

00:14:48.369 --> 00:14:52.730
lead in the US market, but they lead in the
China market and the worldwide market. And

00:14:52.730 --> 00:14:57.379
a lot of the electric vehicle manufacturers
does by Tesla are there. So we don't want

00:14:57.379 --> 00:15:02.670
China to eventually be exporting to Europe
and to be exporting to the rest of the world.

00:15:02.670 --> 00:15:07.629
We want our countries companies to do that.
And that's why it has to be collaborative

00:15:07.629 --> 00:15:11.230
with the government and the private sector
to innovate.

00:15:11.230 --> 00:15:15.579
Last thing I want to ask about two structural
issues that we talk about so much in terms

00:15:15.579 --> 00:15:20.279
of how future elections will will work. Those
are campaign finance, how our elections are

00:15:20.279 --> 00:15:25.850
financed and paid for, and also how how we
count votes. Right. The idea of first past

00:15:25.850 --> 00:15:33.890
the post within an electoral college system.
Do you see the possibility of any reform or

00:15:33.890 --> 00:15:38.040
changes on either of those two issues in the
next two years? Is there even an appetite

00:15:38.040 --> 00:15:42.000
for it right now within the Biden administration
and the Democratic Party?

00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:49.189
Well, H.R. one is all about the and a big
ideas to give individuals matching funds to

00:15:49.189 --> 00:15:55.551
be able to contribute to eliminate big, big
money to help have redistricting reform. I

00:15:55.551 --> 00:15:59.309
think it'll pass the House again. The question
becomes what happens in the Senate? And then

00:15:59.309 --> 00:16:03.949
the question becomes what parts of the Roberts
court hold constitutional? My view is that

00:16:03.949 --> 00:16:08.360
the simplest thing is to make every voter
a donor, give every person a fifty dollar

00:16:08.360 --> 00:16:16.129
credit to be able to use on campaigns to democratize
finance and lessen the value of big money.

00:16:16.129 --> 00:16:21.189
Yeah, well, I mean, one interesting piece
I read said that that's a great half measure,

00:16:21.189 --> 00:16:26.069
but ideally you would eliminate all of that
and simply have straight public financing

00:16:26.069 --> 00:16:32.740
of elections. Do you see that democratization
of funds as an intermediate step or is the

00:16:32.740 --> 00:16:35.551
end goal?
I think it is an intermediate step. I mean,

00:16:35.551 --> 00:16:39.889
I would love public financing of elections,
but that just would be struck down as unconstitutional

00:16:39.889 --> 00:16:46.480
under the way this court has held this delay.
How an understanding of money is speech about

00:16:46.480 --> 00:16:51.550
your proposal that Larry Lessig and some others
have come up with is to say that it's constitutional,

00:16:51.550 --> 00:16:57.709
even under a Roberts court. And if we can't
get rid of the billionaires spending money,

00:16:57.709 --> 00:17:03.551
at least we can dwarf them with citizen resources.
And so that's the idea. But obviously, it

00:17:03.551 --> 00:17:07.789
is a half measure.
Absolutely. Last thing we just learned about

00:17:07.789 --> 00:17:14.500
Joe Biden's plan to essentially pack the Postal
Service Board of governors, adding three individuals

00:17:14.500 --> 00:17:19.579
so that then Lewis Dejoy could be removed.
Does that sound to you like it will happen

00:17:19.579 --> 00:17:25.279
as smoothly and as quickly as some are hoping?
It should. I hope so. I applaud the president

00:17:25.279 --> 00:17:29.620
for doing that. I mean, look, Joy was just
a disaster, not just because of his trying

00:17:29.620 --> 00:17:34.760
to play games with the election, but his view
that you would basically privatize the postal

00:17:34.760 --> 00:17:39.520
service and that that was never the intention.
When private companies can explain to me how

00:17:39.520 --> 00:17:45.500
they're going to guarantee mail going across
country for 50 cents and the speed it does

00:17:45.500 --> 00:17:49.220
that we could talk. But the reality is they
